TI  - Genefer: Programs for Finding Large Probable Generalized Fermat Primes
AU  - Bethune, Iain Arthur
AU  - Gallot, Yves
T2  - Journal of Open Research Software
DA  - 2015/11/19/
PY  - 2015
DO  - 10.5334/jors.ca
DP  - Crossref
VL  - 3
IS  - 1
SP  - 10
J2  - JORS
SN  - 2049-9647
ST  - Genefer
UR  - https://openresearchsoftware.metajnl.com/article/10.5334/jors.ca/
Y2  - 2022/11/20/
L1  - https://openresearchsoftware.metajnl.com/articles/77/files/submission/proof/77-1-759-2-10-20151124.pdf
L4  - https://openresearchsoftware.metajnl.com/articles/10.5334/jors.ca/galley/110/download/
N1  - <p>Abstract: "Genefer has been extensively used by PrimeGrid - a volunteer computing project searching for large prime numbers of various kinds, including GFNs." Introduction: "Since 2009, the PrimeGrid volunteer computing project has used Genefer to extend the search to well over a million digits and is currently searching for a world-record sized GFN prime." Also states "support for BOINC has been added, which enables Genefer to be used by many thousands of users transparently via the BOINC client" and "The released binaries are then distributed to PrimeGrid users via BOINC." References cite the PrimeGrid platform paper and the PPAM Generalized Fermat paper.</p>
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - PrimeGrid: Searching for a New World Record Prime Number
AU  - Bethune, Iain
T2  - Mathematics Today
DA  - 2013/12//
PY  - 2013
VL  - 49
IS  - 6
SP  - 250
EP  - 251
UR  - https://ibethune.github.io/files/PrimeGrid_MT_Dec2013.pdf
L1  - https://ibethune.github.io/files/PrimeGrid_MT_Dec2013.pdf
N1  - <p>Article about the PrimeGrid volunteer computing project: "PrimeGrid is a volunteer computing project which has two main aims; firstly to find large prime numbers, and secondly to educate members of the project and the wider public about the mathematics of primes." Reports "as of November 2013, over 70% of the primes on the Top 5000 list of largest known primes were discovered by PrimeGrid" and records for the largest known Cullen and Woodall primes, largest known Twin primes and Sophie Germain pairs, and longest arithmetic progression (26 primes). Describes the Generalized Fermat Number search via PrimeGrid and participation via the BOINC Manager. References cite the PrimeGrid website and the PPAM Generalized Fermat paper.</p>
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - An Efficient Modular Exponentiation Proof Scheme
AU  - Li, Darren
AU  - Gallot, Yves
T2  - arXiv:2209.15623 [cs.CR]
DA  - 2022/09/16/
PY  - 2022
DO  - 10.48550/arXiv.2209.15623
UR  - https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.15623
L1  - http://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.15623v2
N1  - <p>Abstract: proof scheme "has been successfully implemented at PrimeGrid, doubling the efficiency of distributed searches for primes." Introduction: "The majority of ongoing organized searches for primes of record-breaking sizes, such as PrimeGrid, are distributed." Section 5.3 "Implementation at PrimeGrid": "Our proof scheme and its corresponding verification process has been successfully implemented at PrimeGrid, doubling the speed of PrimeGrid's search that spans hundreds of thousands of computers worldwide. Among others, the search for the world's largest prime on PrimeGrid now uses our proof scheme." and "The first prime found using our work is 117687318131072 + 1, with 1057847 digits, discovered by Tom Greer." Acknowledgements thank the PrimeGrid administrators for integration into the PrimeGrid servers. References cite the PrimeGrid website and the Genefer paper.</p>
